<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lundu</h1><p class="st-meta">1590 CE – 1800 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Manganja kingdom of the lower Shire valley, a southern offshoot of the Maravi confederacy linked to the feared Zimba warriors.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Lundu title was held by a branch of the Phiri ruling clan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-maravi">Kingdom of Maravi</a>, which settled in the lower Shire valley among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manganja">Manganja</a>. By the late 16th century the Lundu was a powerful independent ruler, and Portuguese writers associated him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimba">Zimba</a>, warrior bands that attacked Portuguese posts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> and coastal towns.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Lundu controlled routes carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> from the interior to the coast near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quelimane">Quelimane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique-island">Mozambique Island</a> and drew tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manganja">Manganja</a> chiefs. Religious authority was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbona">Mbona</a> rain shrine at Khulubvi, a cult the Lundu dynasty is said to have taken over and reshaped.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom was brought back under Maravi overlordship by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalonga-masula">Kalonga Masula</a> in the early 17th century and gradually lost power to chiefs and Afro-Portuguese prazo holders. By around 1800 it had broken up, and 19th-century <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kololo-kingdom">Kololo</a> and Yao incursions completed its decline.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1590 CE – 1800 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.34%;width:4.18%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1590 CE – 1800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Chewa-Manganja religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chimang'anja</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a>, <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lundu, Manganja kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1695&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lundu" aria-label="Kingdom of Lundu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5597 2680 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lundu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5956 2992h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5917 2936h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5917 2936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Malawi. <a href="/atlas?year=1695&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lundu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
